GUIDES_INTRO = ("Practical planning notes for shop owners and tenants in Singapore. Each guide explains one decision, such as briefing a fit-out, choosing shelving materials or planning a counter, in plain language you can use before talking to any contractor. They are general guidance, so always confirm details for your own unit.")

G1 = {
  'path': '/guides/how-to-brief-a-shop-fit-out/',
  'title': 'How to Brief a Shop Fit-Out | R&C',
  'desc': 'A practical checklist for briefing a shop fit-out in Singapore: what to prepare before you contact anyone, and how to compare quotations on the same scope.',
  'h1': 'How to Brief a Shop Fit-Out',
  'kicker': 'Planning guide',
  'answer': 'A good fit-out brief tells a contractor what the space is for, what must be ready and by when, which rules apply and where your money matters most. Prepare a floor plan, unit photos, intended use, target opening date, landlord rules and budget priorities, then compare quotations on identical scope.',
  'card': 'What to prepare before you speak to any contractor, and how to compare shop fit-out quotations on like-for-like scope.',
  'image': 'design-planning',
  'image_alt': 'A floor plan and material samples laid out on a table for planning a shop fit-out (AI design concept)',
  'date': '2026-10-24',
  'owner': '/retail-commercial-design-build/',
  'reading': '6 min read',
  'sections': [
    {'h2': 'Why does a written brief matter?', 'html': '<p>A written brief gives every contractor the same starting information, so the quotations you receive describe the same job. Without one, each contractor fills the gaps with their own assumptions, and the differences in price and scope become hard to explain.</p><p>Keep the brief honest about what you do not know. Unknowns such as ceiling condition, existing wiring or landlord restrictions are better listed as questions than hidden, because they often affect scope and cost later.</p><p>A brief does not need to be technical. One or two pages in plain language, plus a few attachments, is usually enough to start a useful conversation. It also forces you to decide what you actually need before spending time on site visits.</p><p>The brief is a working document. Expect it to change after the site assessment, but keep a dated copy so you can see what was agreed and what moved.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What should I prepare before contacting a contractor?', 'html': '<p>Prepare six things: a floor plan, photos of the unit, how the space will be used, your target opening date, the landlord or mall fit-out rules, and your budget priorities. Even rough versions let a contractor ask better questions and give you a more useful first response.</p><ol><li><strong>Floor plan.</strong> The landlord\'s unit plan or a hand-drawn sketch with measurements. Mark the entrance, columns, existing power points and any fixed items.</li><li><strong>Unit photos.</strong> Ceiling, floor, walls, shopfront, the existing lighting and the view from the entrance. Include anything you are unsure about.</li><li><strong>Intended use.</strong> What you sell, how customers move through the space, how many staff work there, and what must be stored on site.</li><li><strong>Target opening date.</strong> The date you hope to open, and whether it is fixed (for example a stock launch) or flexible.</li><li><strong>Landlord or mall rules.</strong> Any fit-out guide, handover condition or contractor requirement you have been given. Check with your landlord or mall management about approvals, permitted working hours and contractor rules.</li><li><strong>Budget priorities.</strong> Not just a total, but which items matter most: for example the counter, display fixtures, lighting or flooring.</li></ol>'},
    {'h2': 'How do I describe how the space will be used?', 'html': '<p>Describe the customer journey and the daily routine in plain words: where people enter, what they see first, where they pay, and where staff pack, store and work. This helps a contractor suggest layouts and joinery that fit how you trade, not just how the unit looks.</p><ul><li>What products you display, and how many lines you expect to carry.</li><li>Which items need to be reachable by customers and which stay behind the counter.</li><li>Whether stock is replenished daily or in larger batches.</li><li>Any equipment that needs power or data, such as a card terminal, printer, screen or speakers.</li><li>Any changes you expect in future, such as seasonal displays or a second counter.</li></ul><p>If you are not sure, say so. A brief that states open questions is more useful than one that guesses.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'Which budget priorities should I share?', 'html': '<p>Share a ranked list of what matters most, and say which items can be simplified if costs rise. A total figure alone does not tell a contractor whether to spend on custom joinery, lighting or finishes. Priorities help them propose options that suit your business.</p><p>Costs in a fit-out are usually driven by scope, materials, the amount of custom work, site access and working-hour limits, the number of trades involved and the finish level. Naming your top two or three priorities keeps those trade-offs visible.</p><p class="note">Tip: separate "must have for opening" from "nice to add later". Phased items, such as extra display units or feature lighting, can often be planned for now and installed afterwards.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'How do I compare quotations on like-for-like scope?', 'html': '<p>Put the quotations side by side and check that each one covers the same items, materials, finishes and exclusions. A lower total can simply mean something is left out. Ask each contractor to clarify any line that is vague, then compare again once the scope matches.</p><p>Use this checklist when reading each quotation:</p><ol><li>Is every item from your brief listed, or marked as excluded?</li><li>Are materials and finishes named, or described only as "as per discussion"?</li><li>Does it state who handles electrical points, lighting, flooring and painting?</li><li>Are approvals, submissions and landlord coordination included or left to you?</li><li>Are site protection, cleaning and rubbish removal covered?</li><li>How are changes to scope handled once work has started?</li><li>What is the payment structure, and what is it tied to?</li><li>What is assumed about site access and working hours?</li></ol><p>A simple table with one column per contractor and one row per scope item makes gaps obvious.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What should I ask at the site visit?', 'html': '<p>Use the visit to confirm the things photos cannot show. Ask what the contractor noticed about the ceiling, floor level, power supply and access route, and what they would need to verify before finalising a quotation. Take notes, and ask for anything agreed on site to be confirmed in writing afterwards.</p><ul><li>Which items they would check or measure again before ordering materials.</li><li>Which works may need submissions by qualified professionals; these should be identified at the site assessment stage.</li><li>How deliveries, lift use and noisy work would be scheduled around the building rules.</li><li>What is likely to affect the schedule, such as lead times for custom items or other trades.</li></ul><p>Be wary of any answer that sounds certain before the site has been seen.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What happens after the brief is agreed?', 'html': '<p>Once the brief and scope are agreed, the usual sequence is layout confirmation, material and finish selection, a written scope and quotation, landlord submissions where needed, works on site, then handover and a defects check. Each step is easier when the brief is still current.</p><p>An R&amp;C project is planned to follow this same sequence, starting with your brief and a site assessment. If you would like help shaping the brief, see the <a href="/retail-commercial-design-build/">design and build planning page</a>, or send us your floor plan through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and we\'ll discuss your scope.</p>'},
  ],
  'faqs': [
    ('Do I need drawings before I contact a contractor?', 'No. A landlord unit plan or a hand sketch with approximate measurements is enough to start. Add photos and a short description of how you trade. The contractor will confirm measurements at the site assessment before preparing anything final.'),
    ('Should I tell contractors my budget?', 'It helps to share your priorities and a working budget, so options can be matched to what you can spend. If you prefer not to give a figure, at least rank what matters most so the scope reflects your choices.'),
    ('How many quotations should I collect?', 'There is no fixed number. Collecting a few lets you spot differences in scope and assumptions, but only if each one is based on the same brief. Comparing too many unlike quotations can make the decision harder.'),
    ('Who handles landlord approvals?', 'It varies by building and by contract. Check with your landlord or mall management about approvals, permitted working hours and contractor rules, and ask each contractor to state in writing what they will and will not coordinate.'),
  ],
  'related': ['/retail-commercial-design-build/', '/guides/small-shop-renovation-checklist/', '/guides/planning-a-custom-shop-counter/', '/spaces/retail-shops-boutiques/'],
  'wa_topic': 'briefing a shop fit-out',
}

G2 = {
  'path': '/guides/retail-display-shelving-materials/',
  'title': 'Retail Display Shelving Materials | R&C',
  'desc': 'Compare laminate, veneer, solid wood, metal, glass and acrylic for shop shelving: durability, load, cleaning, look and what drives cost.',
  'h1': 'Retail Display Shelving Materials Compared',
  'kicker': 'Materials guide',
  'answer': 'The right shelving material depends on what it holds, how often it is touched and cleaned, and the look you want. Laminate suits busy shops, veneer and solid wood add warmth, metal handles load, and glass or acrylic keep displays light. Many shops combine two or three materials.',
  'card': 'Laminate, veneer, solid wood, metal, glass and acrylic compared for durability, load, cleaning, look and cost drivers.',
  'image': 'display-shelving',
  'image_alt': 'Wall-mounted retail display shelving in mixed timber and metal finishes (AI design concept)',
  'date': '2026-10-24',
  'owner': '/services/commercial-carpentry/',
  'reading': '6 min read',
  'sections': [
    {'h2': 'How do I choose a shelving material for my shop?', 'html': '<p>Start with the product, not the finish. Consider the weight of what you display, how often customers handle it, how the shelf will be cleaned and what look suits your brand. Then compare materials against those four questions before thinking about colour or texture.</p><p>Shelving is one of the most visible parts of a shop, and one of the hardest to change once installed, so a clear idea of use helps avoid costly changes later. It also helps to separate shelves into zones. A feature wall near the entrance can justify a more decorative material, while stock shelving at the back can prioritise strength and easy cleaning.</p><ol><li>List what each shelf will carry and roughly how heavy it is.</li><li>Note how much handling and cleaning it will see each day.</li><li>Decide the visual tone: warm, clean, industrial or minimal.</li><li>Check the budget priority for that zone.</li><li>Confirm the choice against site conditions, such as humidity and wall type.</li></ol>'},
    {'h2': 'What are laminate and veneer shelves like?', 'html': '<p>Laminate is a printed, hard-wearing surface bonded to a board, and veneer is a thin layer of real timber on a board. Laminate resists scuffs and wipes clean easily. Veneer gives a natural grain and warmer look but needs more careful handling and finishing.</p><h3>Laminate</h3><ul><li>Wide range of colours and textures, including timber looks.</li><li>Generally durable and easy to wipe down.</li><li>Edges and joins need good detailing, as exposed edges can chip or lift over time.</li></ul><h3>Veneer</h3><ul><li>Real wood grain, which varies from sheet to sheet.</li><li>Can be stained or finished to match other timber in the shop.</li><li>Surface is thin, so deep scratches are harder to hide and repair.</li></ul><p>Both depend heavily on the board underneath and on edge and joint quality, which is where a lot of the difference in cost and lifespan comes from.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'When is solid wood worth considering?', 'html': '<p>Solid wood suits shelves where a natural, substantial look matters and the surface will take regular contact. It can be sanded and refinished, which helps over a long period, but it costs more, moves with humidity and needs thoughtful detailing to avoid warping.</p><ul><li>Good for feature shelves, counter tops and statement pieces.</li><li>Species, grade and finish strongly affect both look and cost.</li><li>Long spans may need extra support or thicker sections.</li><li>Sealed finishes help with cleaning and everyday wear.</li></ul><p>Many shops use solid wood only on visible edges or key surfaces and pair it with board materials elsewhere to control cost.</p><p>Ask which species and grade are being quoted, how the timber is dried and sealed, and how the finish can be repaired. These details explain much of the difference between two solid wood quotations.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What can metal shelving do that timber cannot?', 'html': '<p>Metal handles heavier loads and longer spans with slimmer profiles than most timber or board. It suits stock-heavy displays, industrial looks and adjustable systems. The trade-offs are visual coldness in some settings, sharp edges if poorly finished, and the need for a durable coating.</p><ul><li>Powder-coated or brushed finishes can be matched to your palette.</li><li>Thin steel brackets and frames keep displays visually light.</li><li>Fixing method matters: the wall must be able to carry the load.</li><li>Fingerprints and scratches show differently depending on the finish.</li></ul><p>Confirm wall construction on site before choosing wall-mounted metal systems for heavy items. Landlord rules may also limit drilling into certain walls, so raise this early.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'Are glass and acrylic suitable for display shelves?', 'html': '<p>Yes, for lighter, high-visibility displays such as cosmetics, jewellery, watches and small accessories. Glass looks clear and stays clean-looking but is heavy and breakable. Acrylic is lighter and more impact-resistant but scratches more easily and can yellow or cloud with wear.</p><h3>Glass</h3><ul><li>Clear and refined, with good light transmission.</li><li>Needs safe edge treatment and proper fixings.</li><li>Fingermarks show quickly, so cleaning routines matter.</li></ul><h3>Acrylic</h3><ul><li>Lighter and easier to shape, including curved and cut pieces.</li><li>Scratches can build up with frequent wiping or abrasive cloths.</li><li>Use cleaning products suitable for the material.</li></ul><p class="note">Glass or acrylic shelves can be paired with feature lighting to make products stand out. See the <a href="/guides/lighting-a-retail-display/">lighting guide</a> for planning.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'How should I plan for cleaning and wear?', 'html': '<p>Plan cleaning before you choose the finish. Ask what your staff will use, how often shelves are wiped and whether stock rubs against the surface. A material that suits a gift shop may wear quickly in a shop where heavy cartons are moved daily.</p><ul><li>Choose finishes that tolerate the cleaning products your staff already use.</li><li>Consider protective edging where trolleys or boxes will knock shelves.</li><li>Keep a small sample of the chosen finish for matching touch-ups later.</li><li>Think about humidity: some board and timber materials respond differently in damp conditions.</li><li>Allow adjustable shelves where product ranges change often.</li></ul><p>Adjustability is often worth planning in from the start, because shops rarely stock the same range in the same way for long.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What drives the cost of shelving?', 'html': '<p>Cost depends less on the material name and more on how it is used. Thickness, size, custom shapes, edge detailing, hardware, finish quality and installation conditions usually matter as much as the base material. Comparing quotations only by material name can give the wrong picture.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Cost driver</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custom sizes</td><td>More cutting and fitting time</td></tr><tr><td>Edge and joint detail</td><td>Affects durability and look</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Load rating and adjustability</td></tr><tr><td>Finish</td><td>Coatings and sealing add steps</td></tr><tr><td>Site access</td><td>Lifts, hours and wall type</td></tr></tbody></table>'},
    {'h2': 'Which combination works for most small shops?', 'html': '<p>A common approach is a durable board-based carcass in laminate or veneer, with metal or timber accents on visible edges and glass or acrylic only where products need extra visibility. This balances daily wear, appearance and budget without relying on one material for everything.</p><p>A commercial carpentry scope usually covers shelving design, cutting, finishing and installation, while lighting, flooring and painting are often separate scopes. For more on what is typically included, see the <a href="/services/commercial-carpentry/">commercial carpentry page</a>, or send us your floor plan and we\'ll discuss your scope.</p>'},
  ],
  'faqs': [
    ('Which shelving material is easiest to clean?', 'Laminate and sealed metal are usually the easiest to wipe down. Glass cleans well but shows fingermarks, and acrylic needs gentle cloths and suitable products to avoid scratches. Always confirm cleaning instructions for the finish you choose, and ask what products are suitable before your staff start using them.'),
    ('Can I mix materials on the same wall?', 'Yes, and many shops do. Mixing is common, for example timber-look laminate with metal brackets or glass shelves. Keep the number of materials limited so the display looks consistent, and check that fixings suit each material.'),
    ('How do I know how much weight a shelf can carry?', 'Load depends on the material, thickness, span, bracket type and the wall behind it. Tell the contractor what you plan to display, and ask them to confirm the intended load and fixing method on site before ordering.'),
    ('Is solid wood better than laminate?', 'Neither is better in every case. Solid wood offers a natural look and can be refinished, while laminate is practical for busy areas and easier to maintain. The better choice depends on use, cleaning and your priorities.'),
  ],
  'related': ['/services/commercial-carpentry/', '/guides/lighting-a-retail-display/', '/spaces/retail-shops-boutiques/', '/guides/how-to-brief-a-shop-fit-out/'],
  'wa_topic': 'display shelving for my shop',
}

G3 = {
  'path': '/guides/planning-a-custom-shop-counter/',
  'title': 'Planning a Custom Shop Counter | R&C',
  'desc': 'Plan a custom shop counter: functions, typical heights as starting points, accessibility, materials, power and data points, and what to prepare.',
  'h1': 'Planning a Custom Shop Counter',
  'kicker': 'Planning guide',
  'answer': 'Plan a shop counter around what happens at it: payment, packing, display, storage and cables. Decide the functions first, then the size, height, materials and power or data points. Typical dimensions are only starting points, so confirm them with your staff, equipment and the actual space.',
  'card': 'How to plan a custom shop counter: functions, typical heights, accessibility, materials, and power and data points.',
  'image': 'counter-joinery',
  'image_alt': 'Close-up of a custom timber shop counter with a built-in payment area (AI design concept)',
  'date': '2026-10-24',
  'owner': '/services/commercial-carpentry/',
  'reading': '6 min read',
  'sections': [
    {'h2': 'What should a shop counter do?', 'html': '<p>A shop counter usually handles several jobs at once: taking payment, wrapping or packing items, displaying products, storing supplies and hiding cables. Listing these jobs before design begins prevents a counter that looks good but forces staff to work around it every day.</p><ol><li><strong>Payment.</strong> Space for the terminal, a receipt printer and a cash drawer if you use one.</li><li><strong>Packing.</strong> A clear surface for bags, tissue, boxes and tape, ideally within arm\'s reach.</li><li><strong>Display.</strong> Impulse items, a showcase section or promotional space.</li><li><strong>Storage.</strong> Shelves or drawers for bags, stationery, spare stock and cleaning items.</li><li><strong>Cables.</strong> Routes for power and data that stay hidden but remain reachable.</li></ol><p>Ask your staff what slows them down at the moment. Their answers are often the most useful design input.</p><p>If you are a cafe or service business, add the items specific to you, such as a pick-up shelf, a queue rail or space for a display chiller. Food licensing and kitchen compliance remain the operator\'s own matter and are outside a counter joinery scope.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'How do I decide the size and layout?', 'html': '<p>Work out the size from the equipment, the number of staff and the queue space in front of it, then check against the unit plan. A counter that is too large crowds the entrance, while one that is too small pushes work onto the shelves behind it.</p><ul><li>Mark where customers will queue and where doors swing.</li><li>Place the counter where staff can see the entrance and the shop floor.</li><li>Allow staff to move behind it without turning sideways.</li><li>Consider whether one or two staff will use it at peak times.</li><li>Leave room for future equipment, such as a second terminal or label printer.</li><li>Check that drawers and doors can open fully without hitting walls or stools.</li></ul><p>Also think about what customers see. A tidy front, a place to rest a bag and a clear spot to tap a card all make the counter feel easier to use.</p><p>Cut a cardboard or tape outline on the floor of the unit. Standing behind it for a few minutes often reveals problems that a drawing hides.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What heights are typical for a counter?', 'html': '<p>Many service counters sit around 900-1,050 mm high, but treat this as a starting point to confirm with your staff and equipment. Standing staff, seated staff, tall terminals and customer comfort can all change the right height for your shop.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Common starting point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main counter top</td><td>About 900-1,050 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Lower section for lower reach</td><td>Often lower than the main top</td></tr><tr><td>Depth for terminal and packing</td><td>Confirm against equipment</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="note">Mock up the height with a table or boxes and ask your staff to work at it for a few minutes. Confirm final dimensions on site before production.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'How do I make a counter accessible?', 'html': '<p>Include at least one lower or clear section where customers who use a wheelchair, or who find a higher surface difficult, can pay and be served comfortably. Think about reach, knee space, and clear approach as well as the height of the counter top.</p><ul><li>A lower section or a pull-out surface alongside the main counter.</li><li>A clear approach path without steps or displays in the way.</li><li>Card terminals positioned where they can be reached and read easily.</li><li>Good lighting on the payment area for reading screens and receipts.</li></ul><p>Requirements differ between buildings and uses. Check with your landlord or mall management about approvals and any rules that affect your layout, and confirm the details during the site assessment.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'Which materials suit a shop counter?', 'html': '<p>A counter takes more knocks, spills and wiping than most fixtures, so choose surfaces for durability and cleaning first, then appearance. Laminate, veneered timber, solid surface, timber, stone-look boards and metal are all common, each with different wear, cleaning and maintenance trade-offs that are worth comparing before you commit.</p><p>Think about the surface where staff place bags and drinks, the edge customers lean on, and the front panel that gets kicked.</p><ul><li><strong>Laminate:</strong> practical, wide range of looks, easy to wipe.</li><li><strong>Timber or veneer:</strong> warm and natural, needs a sealed, protective finish.</li><li><strong>Solid surface or stone-look:</strong> seamless, hard-wearing, cost depends on thickness and fabrication.</li><li><strong>Metal:</strong> strong edge protection or feature panels, shows marks differently by finish.</li></ul><p>Many counters mix a tough top surface with a different material on the customer-facing front. For more on shelving and fixtures, see the <a href="/guides/retail-display-shelving-materials/">materials guide</a>.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'Where do power and data points go?', 'html': '<p>Plan power and data points before the counter is built, so cables run through the joinery rather than across the floor. Mark where each device sits, then ask for outlets and cable openings positioned near them, inside the counter body and accessible for servicing.</p><ul><li>Terminal, printer, scanner, screen and charging needs.</li><li>Data connection for payment and inventory systems.</li><li>Cable openings and routes that avoid sharp edges and pinch points.</li><li>Ventilation if equipment sits inside a closed cabinet.</li><li>Access panels for future changes.</li></ul><p>Electrical points and wiring are generally a separate scope from carpentry. Some works may need submissions by qualified professionals; these should be identified at the site assessment stage. See <a href="/services/commercial-electrical-works/">commercial electrical works</a> for how this is normally split.</p>'},
    {'h2': 'What should I prepare before asking for a quotation?', 'html': '<p>Bring a floor plan, unit photos, a list of equipment with approximate sizes, your preferred materials and your target opening date. With these, a contractor can propose a counter layout and identify what needs to be confirmed on site. Also mention any brand look you want to match, such as colours, logos or existing fixtures.</p><p>An R&amp;C project is planned to begin with a site assessment, followed by layout and material discussion. If you would like to talk through your counter, send us your floor plan and we\'ll discuss your scope. You can also read the <a href="/services/commercial-carpentry/">commercial carpentry page</a> for what this scope usually covers.</p>'},
  ],
  'faqs': [
    ('Should a counter be built-in or freestanding?', 'Built-in counters use space efficiently and hide cables neatly, but are harder to move. Freestanding units can be relocated or reused, but may need more care with cables and stability. Consider how long you plan to stay and whether the layout may change.'),
    ('How do I plan for card terminals and printers?', 'List each device with its approximate size and cable needs, then ask for dedicated openings and power points near its position. Check whether the equipment supplier has specific mounting or ventilation requirements before the joinery is finalised.'),
    ('Do I need a lower section on my counter?', 'It is a useful consideration for customer comfort and accessibility, and many shops include one. Whether it is required depends on your building and use, so check with your landlord or mall management and confirm during the site assessment.'),
    ('Can I add display shelves or glass to a counter?', 'Yes, many counters include a display section or showcase. Consider lighting, cleaning, glass edges and security of stock, and make sure the display part does not block sight lines between staff and customers.'),
  ],
  'related': ['/services/commercial-carpentry/', '/guides/retail-display-shelving-materials/', '/services/commercial-electrical-works/', '/spaces/cafes-service-counters/', '/spaces/retail-shops-boutiques/'],
  'wa_topic': 'a custom shop counter',
}

